  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The velocity $v$ (in $\\text{m/s}$) of a particle at time $t$ (in seconds) is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t+1.$ Calculate the velocity of the particle when the acceleration $a$ is equal to $2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t>0.$\n\nWhen $a=2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ the velocity is $v=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m/s}.$",
  "worked_solution": "We differentiate $v(t)$ to calculate $a(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+2t+1) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-6t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=2,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =3{t}^{2}-6t+2 \\\\ 0 & =3{t}^{2}-6t \\\\ 3t(t-2) & =0 \\\\ t & =0,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $t=2$ since $t>0.$\n\nThen, to calculate the velocity at time $t=2,$ we substitute this into the equation for the velocity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}+2(2)+1 \\\\  & =8-12+4+1 \\\\  & =1\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity when $a=2\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ is $v=1\\,\\text{m/s}.$",
